umdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkf200000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;hYpqZCFyWh9GWlpiL9MwoE78GjmYKrjk2yUtR6YpHvwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarki0.3256614ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ZzwWpifB-pQURswsS5HRtpqfbSK3uUemqZYHeY6BSz0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka2ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ZlUdbGTdCVuzJ44xMldBNDz8baTryfJ8HrithJnh3Gwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke32000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;w54-BNvPl-cQTdAdtaI4dQlJD9vLTuPcaGaYl9iSxywƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd7549ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;f4R3GrMlPkj7GpPybpoXj_tvkWKhF6Z0THaYFF9abc8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jywr5Lpjzu7hX8OjfEsELjKLmVE9TXW3Sat00fPnm60ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka2ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;vVbxnEGgaxXRwA4Z7LWX8yN6Xi9m7kqVdP1VgUNuEhsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;F3GYjmJLsnek04M_bH588etiw-ivUwwcaPOsH3cRCVgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky(ˆ{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"Re-draft from writer-model stage using the existing research packet; sources and assignment do not require replay.","summary":"The sources and assignment are sound, and the deterministic gate cleared with warnings only. However, the writer model produced a draft with eight bounded, repairable defects: prohibited \"will\" in the lede; an attribution-formula construction used approximately ten times against a hard limit of two; only two body sections against the required three to five; five near-duplicate proximity pairs identified by the gate; a headline that elevates \"Messi's Magic\" to coequal status with geometry contrary to the primary source's organizing frame; a misattributed \"speed of reconfiguration/recognition\" phrase credited to the supporting context article rather than the geometry source that coined it; a direct citation to a Reuters URL not in the admitted sources; and a summary field that copies the lede rather than forming a distinct synopsis. All defects are bounded prose repairs; no new reporting is required.","requiredRepairs":["Remove both instances of 'will' from body prose ('will meet' in lede; scan for any others); replace with 'are set to,' 'are scheduled to,' or a present-tense factual construction per the absolute prohibition on 'will' outside a quoted source title.","Reduce the attribution-formula constructions ('as noted in,' 'according to,' 'as described,' 'per the source,' 'as suggested by,' 'as analyzed by,' etc.) from the current ~10 occurrences to no more than two; vary attribution through inline naming, relative clauses, or appositives so the prose does not read as a parade of footnote brackets.","Add at least one additional ## section so the article contains three to five sections as required; the current two-section structure leaves the argument truncated and does not meet the form requirement.","Resolve the five near-duplicate proximity pairs flagged by the copy-editing gate: in each flagged paragraph remove or rewrite the sentence that restates a noun phrase or framing already used in the immediately preceding sentence.","Revise the headline so geometry remains the primary organizing frame consistent with the primary source's own title and argument; 'Messi's Magic' elevated as coequal to geometry misrepresents the article's actual focus and draws from a supporting context article rather than the commissioned story.","Correct the attribution of the 'speed of reconfiguration / speed of recognition' framing: this phrase originates in the geometry article (in reference to France, not Argentina); if extended to the final, it must cite the geometry article, not the Tactically Complete supporting context article, and must carry appropriate hedging to mark the extension.","Replace the direct hyperlink to reuters.com (MbappÃ© 'lack of communication' quote) with a citation to the primary geometry source, which already carries and links to that quotation; reuters.com is not an admitted source in the research packet.","Rewrite the summary field as a standalone three-sentence synopsis (the event, the key numbers, the analytical frame) distinct from the lede; the current summary is a near-verbatim repetition of the lede's first two sentences."],"report":"**Assignment and sources â€” no defect.** The primary source (Conor Boland's \"Geometry, football and the World Cup final,\" The Conversation, 2026-07-17) is well-matched to the slug and SEO term. The supporting context article (\"'Tactically complete' Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina,\" The Conversation, 2026-07-17) is legitimately used for tournament context and tactical contrast. The assignment does not overreach the evidence. No replay is needed at the sources or assignment layer.\n\n**Deterministic standards â€” warnings only, no hard failure.** The gate returned publishable: true with six warnings: one attention-preserve-modality flag on the lede, and five near-duplicate-proximity flags across the body. These are real defects but are repairable without a new research run.\n\n**Writer-model defects â€” eight bounded repairs required.**\n\n*1. Prohibited \"will\" in body prose.* The lede opens \"Spain and Argentina will meet at MetLife Stadium.\" The writing instructions are absolute: \"NEVER use the word 'will' outside a quoted source title.\" Although this refers to a scheduled event rather than a prediction, the prohibition is unconditional. The lede must be restructured (e.g., \"Spain and Argentina are set to meetâ€¦\" or recast in present tense).\n\n*2. Attribution formula overuse.* The manuscript applies the functional equivalents of \"As [X] notes/reports\" approximately ten times: \"as described in recent analysis,\" \"as noted in The Conversation,\" \"per academic reporting,\" \"as reported by The Conversation,\" \"according to the same source,\" \"as detailed in The Conversation,\" \"according to the reporting,\" \"as suggested by analysis,\" \"per the source,\" \"as analyzed by the source.\" The writing instructions cap this construction at two occurrences per article. The draft is almost entirely stitched together with these boilerplate attribution brackets, which drain the prose of specificity and voice without adding clarity.\n\n*3. Only two body sections.* The form requirement is three to five ## sections that build a single analysis. The draft delivers two: \"The geometry of football triangles\" and \"The geometry of the final showdown.\" Both section headings also repeat the word \"geometry of,\" a near-duplicate in the subheadings themselves. A third section is required; the research packet contains sufficient material (e.g., the probability-map / player-tracking dimension, or the basketball analogy as a standalone analytical pivot).\n\n*4. Five near-duplicate proximity pairs.* The deterministic gate identified five specific paragraph pairs where sentence-level phrases repeat within close proximity. Representative examples: the two-sentence lede reintroduces the same match frame twice; the Spain descriptive paragraph pairs \"patient possession and defensive organization\" with \"Six different players have scored for the Spanish squad\" in a way that restates team-wide excellence without advancing the argument; the Argentina paragraph pairs the Messi-sacrifice model description with \"This model has served Argentina in high-pressure matches\" as a restatement; and the closing paragraph repeats the collision-of-approaches framing twice. Each must be resolved by removing or rewriting the redundant sentence.\n\n*5. Headline misrepresents the primary source's frame.* \"Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown\" introduces \"Messi's Magic\" as coequal to geometry. The primary source (the geometry article) is organized entirely around Spain's passing-triangle mechanics; Argentina and Messi appear only in the final two sentences as an open question. \"Messi's Magic\" as a headline term is drawn from the supporting context article, which is a separate commissioned piece. Fusing them at the headline level is a faithfulness failure: the article body is predominantly a geometric/tactical explainer using the Spain-France semi-final, not a head-to-head Messi preview. The headline instruction requires preserving the source event's own organizing terms; the geometry article's own title does this and should guide the revision.\n\n*6. Misattributed \"speed of reconfiguration / speed of recognition.\"* The manuscript writes: \"The final may reveal whether Spain's speed of reconfiguration can overcome Argentina's speed of recognition [as suggested by analysis]\" with a link to the Tactically Complete article. But this paired framing (\"Spain's speed of reconfiguration\" / \"France's speed of recognition\") comes verbatim from the geometry article, where it describes the Spainâ€“France semi-final: \"The deeper contest was between Spain's speed of reconfiguration and France's speed of recognition.\" The draft repurposes it to Argentina without crediting the actual source that introduced the phrase, and the extension to Argentina (rather than France) is an inferential leap not licensed by either admitted source. The citation must be corrected to the geometry article, and if the framing is extended to Argentina the draft must signal the extension explicitly.\n\n*7. Direct Reuters citation outside admitted sources.* MbappÃ©'s \"lack of communication\" quote is linked directly to reuters.com. Reuters is not an admitted source in the research packet. The quote appears inside the primary geometry source, which hyperlinks to Reuters internally. The manuscript must cite the geometry article for this claim, not the Reuters URL. Citing a URL not in the admitted sources as if it were an independent source creates an unverifiable attribution chain.\n\n*8. Summary field repeats the lede.* The summary field reads: \"Spain and Argentina will meet at MetLife Stadium for the 2026 FIFA World Cup finalâ€”a fitting finale between the world's two highest-ranked teams, according to reporting in The Conversation. Both teams arrive at this final match undefeated after seven tournament games, though they enter the contest with arguably distinct tactical philosophies as described in recent analysis.\" This is a near-verbatim copy of the lede's first two sentences, not a standalone synopsis. The form exemplar requires a three-sentence summary (the event, the numbers, the frame) distinct from the lede copy.\n\n**Fairness check â€” no allegation, dispute, or adversarial claim.** The article is an analytical sports preview; no person or institution is criticized. No fairness repair is required.\n\n**Sensationalism / stereotypes check.** The word \"Showdown\" in the headline is mildly sensationalist but not a blocking defect on its own; it should be revised as part of the headline faithfulness repair. No stereotyping or unattributed opinion is present in the body.\n\n**Pipeline diagnosis.** The failure layer is writer-model. The sources are appropriate and complete; the assignment is well-scoped; the deterministic gate cleared with warnings; all defects are traceable to prose execution choices rather than thin evidence or structural assignment problems. The eight repairs listed are bounded and do not require new reporting."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;qSEe6MjbyJL_GpCLwPWNoDgYrRVUb9YlwL1mmdPRXVYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$14c134a1-1b4b-4fc3-b1c9-0b243ada37cfƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;0Px1FwTHuCrwTD-KeHZKogpv13IPQFiawIi1V4Gqfvgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhtool_useƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;kqW2wPBlaxGu4Quuw7B4AZb91l0kjNAU04Ffm2U_Pbsƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;n28ZbwvOs_qoaNubzZ-f098wmf5qBx3LtLvT4FWpA7Uƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarklwriter-modelƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;MLvStwvtJmSLv7vmCiqtyqVaBtu3iV2L4GKWmVKqk9Uƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxrRe-draft from writer-model stage using the existing research packet; sources and assignment do not require replay.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;L4K40S86u5myh12CwidxDbpmaViV9J2B9VOaETEH7Hcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky3**Assignment and sources â€” no defect.** The primary source (Conor Boland's "Geometry, football and the World Cup final," The Conversation, 2026-07-17) is well-matched to the slug and SEO term. The supporting context article ("'Tactically complete' Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina," The Conversation, 2026-07-17) is legitimately used for tournament context and tactical contrast. The assignment does not overreach the evidence. No replay is needed at the sources or assignment layer.

**Deterministic standards â€” warnings only, no hard failure.** The gate returned publishable: true with six warnings: one attention-preserve-modality flag on the lede, and five near-duplicate-proximity flags across the body. These are real defects but are repairable without a new research run.

**Writer-model defects â€” eight bounded repairs required.**

*1. Prohibited "will" in body prose.* The lede opens "Spain and Argentina will meet at MetLife Stadium." The writing instructions are absolute: "NEVER use the word 'will' outside a quoted source title." Although this refers to a scheduled event rather than a prediction, the prohibition is unconditional. The lede must be restructured (e.g., "Spain and Argentina are set to meetâ€¦" or recast in present tense).

*2. Attribution formula overuse.* The manuscript applies the functional equivalents of "As [X] notes/reports" approximately ten times: "as described in recent analysis," "as noted in The Conversation," "per academic reporting," "as reported by The Conversation," "according to the same source," "as detailed in The Conversation," "according to the reporting," "as suggested by analysis," "per the source," "as analyzed by the source." The writing instructions cap this construction at two occurrences per article. The draft is almost entirely stitched together with these boilerplate attribution brackets, which drain the prose of specificity and voice without adding clarity.

*3. Only two body sections.* The form requirement is three to five ## sections that build a single analysis. The draft delivers two: "The geometry of football triangles" and "The geometry of the final showdown." Both section headings also repeat the word "geometry of," a near-duplicate in the subheadings themselves. A third section is required; the research packet contains sufficient material (e.g., the probability-map / player-tracking dimension, or the basketball analogy as a standalone analytical pivot).

*4. Five near-duplicate proximity pairs.* The deterministic gate identified five specific paragraph pairs where sentence-level phrases repeat within close proximity. Representative examples: the two-sentence lede reintroduces the same match frame twice; the Spain descriptive paragraph pairs "patient possession and defensive organization" with "Six different players have scored for the Spanish squad" in a way that restates team-wide excellence without advancing the argument; the Argentina paragraph pairs the Messi-sacrifice model description with "This model has served Argentina in high-pressure matches" as a restatement; and the closing paragraph repeats the collision-of-approaches framing twice. Each must be resolved by removing or rewriting the redundant sentence.

*5. Headline misrepresents the primary source's frame.* "Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown" introduces "Messi's Magic" as coequal to geometry. The primary source (the geometry article) is organized entirely around Spain's passing-triangle mechanics; Argentina and Messi appear only in the final two sentences as an open question. "Messi's Magic" as a headline term is drawn from the supporting context article, which is a separate commissioned piece. Fusing them at the headline level is a faithfulness failure: the article body is predominantly a geometric/tactical explainer using the Spain-France semi-final, not a head-to-head Messi preview. The headline instruction requires preserving the source event's own organizing terms; the geometry article's own title does this and should guide the revision.

*6. Misattributed "speed of reconfiguration / speed of recognition."* The manuscript writes: "The final may reveal whether Spain's speed of reconfiguration can overcome Argentina's speed of recognition [as suggested by analysis]" with a link to the Tactically Complete article. But this paired framing ("Spain's speed of reconfiguration" / "France's speed of recognition") comes verbatim from the geometry article, where it describes the Spainâ€“France semi-final: "The deeper contest was between Spain's speed of reconfiguration and France's speed of recognition." The draft repurposes it to Argentina without crediting the actual source that introduced the phrase, and the extension to Argentina (rather than France) is an inferential leap not licensed by either admitted source. The citation must be corrected to the geometry article, and if the framing is extended to Argentina the draft must signal the extension explicitly.

*7. Direct Reuters citation outside admitted sources.* MbappÃ©'s "lack of communication" quote is linked directly to reuters.com. Reuters is not an admitted source in the research packet. The quote appears inside the primary geometry source, which hyperlinks to Reuters internally. The manuscript must cite the geometry article for this claim, not the Reuters URL. Citing a URL not in the admitted sources as if it were an independent source creates an unverifiable attribution chain.

*8. Summary field repeats the lede.* The summary field reads: "Spain and Argentina will meet at MetLife Stadium for the 2026 FIFA World Cup finalâ€”a fitting finale between the world's two highest-ranked teams, according to reporting in The Conversation. Both teams arrive at this final match undefeated after seven tournament games, though they enter the contest with arguably distinct tactical philosophies as described in recent analysis." This is a near-verbatim copy of the lede's first two sentences, not a standalone synopsis. The form exemplar requires a three-sentence summary (the event, the numbers, the frame) distinct from the lede copy.

**Fairness check â€” no allegation, dispute, or adversarial claim.** The article is an analytical sports preview; no person or institution is criticized. No fairness repair is required.

**Sensationalism / stereotypes check.** The word "Showdown" in the headline is mildly sensationalist but not a blocking defect on its own; it should be revised as part of the headline faithfulness repair. No stereotyping or unattributed opinion is present in the body.

**Pipeline diagnosis.** The failure layer is writer-model. The sources are appropriate and complete; the assignment is well-scoped; the deterministic gate cleared with warnings; all defects are traceable to prose execution choices rather than thin evidence or structural assignment problems. The eight repairs listed are bounded and do not require new reporting.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;C-u4ex-ug2RlMxgJSBJ1gJiN5BtmAHoqRgtPhWw_OnEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;HhjtsfdXd8IUPOXMe67AhlOnSuMkUDKmkQq5TNB3q6sƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxøRemove both instances of 'will' from body prose ('will meet' in lede; scan for any others); replace with 'are set to,' 'are scheduled to,' or a present-tense factual construction per the absolute prohibition on 'will' outside a quoted source title.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Mnsyt8jiSOeN9kHZblaZ4kntOJWRHUZhprVzFEGt35sƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyUReduce the attribution-formula constructions ('as noted in,' 'according to,' 'as described,' 'per the source,' 'as suggested by,' 'as analyzed by,' etc.) from the current ~10 occurrences to no more than two; vary attribution through inline naming, relative clauses, or appositives so the prose does not read as a parade of footnote brackets.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;IuhdKNbuWTDI6QrG25yWilhmMLdKqsdkRtdsh7DcySAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxÊAdd at least one additional ## section so the article contains three to five sections as required; the current two-section structure leaves the argument truncated and does not meet the form requirement.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;X7ucVU9Llafoezk8JXAE